  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nSee also https://github.com/w3ctag/design-principles/issues/619\n\nWe currently use the \"disable scripting\" flag to mean a few self-explained things like not running scripts and enabling the `<noscript>` element, but we also use it to mean other things, like disabling lazy loading behvaior, as an extra protection against scroll-spy tracking.\n\nHowever, the intention behind this preference is not properly specified, and adding some specific protections like the lazy-loading one in the HTML standard is insufficient.\n\nFor example, it's possible to create scroll-spies with CSS background images, scroll timelines, carousels and so on, and \"disable scripting\" doesn't protect against that.\n\nSo, we should clarify in the standard, and across standards (as a TAG principle?) what this preference stands for and its scope.\n\nNote that this preference also affects sandboxed iframes with scripting disallowed so it's not only a browser preference.",
